WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.200
The ocean covers over
70% of our planet. 

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.840
And yet, what we know about it
barely scratches the surface. 

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.640
Beneath its swell is a largely
unexplored universe, 

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.560
until recently, beyond
the gaze of human eyes. 

00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:18.406
So why do we know so little
about the ocean?

00:00:18.677 --> 00:00:21.757
 For a start, immense pressure
presents huge challenges

00:00:21.757 --> 00:00:23.433
for divers and equipment alike. 

00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:26.873
In many ways, it’s easier
to send a mission to space. 

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:32.173
But with new technology,
such as submarine robots, 

00:00:32.173 --> 00:00:35.413
this hidden realm is starting
to reveal its secrets. 

00:00:37.056 --> 00:00:38.376
So, what’s down there? 

00:00:38.376 --> 00:00:40.696
Well, there’s water. Lots of it. 

00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:46.863
1,419,120,000 cubic kilometres,

00:00:46.863 --> 00:00:48.903
to be about as precise
as you can be. 

00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:51.523
And in that water, there’s fish -

00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:55.563
the main source of protein
for around three billion people. 

00:00:55.896 --> 00:00:58.016
But there’s a lot more than
just fish down there. 

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.730
Extraordinary, otherworldly creatures
dwell in the depths,

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.810
with new ones
discovered all the time.

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:06.957
Many are gelatinous -

00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:10.102
jellyfish that disintegrate
if you try to catch them in a net.

00:01:11.536 --> 00:01:15.576
In 2020, scientists found
the giant Siphonophore Apolemia,

00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:18.456
an organism made up of millions
of interconnected clones,

00:01:18.456 --> 00:01:22.456
its thin twisting body reminiscent
of a long piece of string. 

00:01:23.639 --> 00:01:27.279
And the ocean floor is far from being
the flat and featureless seabed

00:01:27.279 --> 00:01:28.519
you might imagine. 

00:01:28.519 --> 00:01:30.279
If you were to drain the ocean,

00:01:30.279 --> 00:01:34.639
a landscape would emerge just
as spectacular as anything on land -

00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:36.719
boasting some of the highest peaks,

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:40.359
deepest canyons and longest
river channels on the planet.

00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:43.282
There are even waterfalls
under the sea -

00:01:43.282 --> 00:01:46.242
the largest being
the Denmark Strait cataract.

00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:48.442
Here, the cold waters
of the Greenland Sea

00:01:48.442 --> 00:01:50.442
meet the warmer waters
of the Irminger.

00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:52.162
As the cooler water is forced down,

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:55.322
it creates a giant three and a half
thousand metre drop,

00:01:55.322 --> 00:01:58.522
undetectable to anyone who might
be bobbing about on the surface.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:02.149
And that’s nothing compared
to the chilling 11,000 metre drop

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:06.189
to the bottom of the Mariana Trench -
the deepest place on Earth. 

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:11.143
It was here that, in 2020, scientists
made an alarming discovery.

00:02:11.143 --> 00:02:13.423
At a depth of around 7,000 metres,

00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:16.476
in one of the most remote
and inaccessible crevices on Earth,

00:02:16.476 --> 00:02:19.516
they came across
a new species of crustacean.

00:02:19.516 --> 00:02:21.556
And it had plastic in its stomach.

00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:24.872
They called it Eurythenes plasticus -

00:02:24.872 --> 00:02:25.992
a living reminder that,

00:02:25.992 --> 00:02:28.512
even though we’ve barely begun
to explore the ocean,

00:02:28.512 --> 00:02:31.472
our impact on it is already
being keenly felt. 

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:35.109
In fact, by 2050,

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.749
it’s estimated there could be more
plastic in the sea than fish.

00:02:40.249 --> 00:02:42.529
But it’s not just plastic
that’s a problem.

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:44.329
There are also “dead zones”,

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:47.289
areas with insufficient oxygen
to support marine life.

00:02:48.379 --> 00:02:50.939
These are becoming more common
thanks to pollution. 

00:02:53.532 --> 00:02:56.092
The sad truth is,
when it comes to the ocean,

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:57.532
the reach of human activity

00:02:57.532 --> 00:02:59.852
goes far beyond the reach
of our knowledge.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:03.099
It’s easy to feel detached
from the ocean -

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:05.219
particularly if you live inland.

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:08.459
And this might explain why
we’ve treated it as a dumping ground.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:11.619
But the more we explore,
the more we find it has to offer.

00:03:12.335 --> 00:03:14.895
For example, the gene pool
of deep ocean life,

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:17.015
such as sponges and microorganisms,

00:03:17.015 --> 00:03:19.175
could hold the key to solving
the urgent problem

00:03:19.175 --> 00:03:20.775
of antibiotic resistance. 

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:26.059
More importantly, the ocean is key
to almost all life on the planet.

00:03:26.059 --> 00:03:29.499
Half the oxygen we breathe
comes from marine photosynthesizers

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:32.219
such as phytoplankton
and seaweed.

00:03:33.302 --> 00:03:35.502
The ocean also regulates our climate,

00:03:35.502 --> 00:03:38.982
mediating temperature by distributing
solar heat around the planet.  

00:03:40.059 --> 00:03:43.419
We may not feel it, but every
one of us is affected every day

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:46.659
by the role the ocean plays in our
finely-balanced Earth system.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:49.829
And yet, the efforts
we’ve made so far

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.429
to protect and preserve
this vital life source

00:03:52.429 --> 00:03:55.109
are, well, a drop in the ocean. 

00:03:57.945 --> 00:03:59.585
There’s still so much
we don’t know -

00:03:59.585 --> 00:04:03.985
so many breathtaking canyons unseen,
so many creatures undiscovered - 

00:04:04.026 --> 00:04:08.346
but new technology is revealing more
about our ocean than ever before. 

00:04:08.346 --> 00:04:11.106
Perhaps if we knew more
of the ocean’s secrets

00:04:11.106 --> 00:04:13.466
we might look after it better. 

